# What gets uploaded to Railway for the container build.
#
# This used to exclude `backend/external` and `backend/sensen/external`, which
# CMake requires: external/SGEE is an add_subdirectory target and
# sensen/external/fastestjsoninthewest provides the `fastjson` module. With
# them missing the build could not configure, let alone compile.
#
# The bulk that made them look expensive is not source: SGEE carries a 1.4 GB
# web-lsp tree and a 132 MB VS Code extension, neither of which the engine
# links against. Those are excluded by path instead.

.git
*.log

# Frontend — deployed separately to Cloudflare Pages.
frontend
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Model training / dataset tooling. The container build never references it --
# `grep -c 'agent' backend/Dockerfile` is 0 -- and it is 35 MB of Python and
# datasets that would otherwise ride along on every deploy.
agent

# Build outputs.
#
# `backend/build` alone is NOT enough and its absence broke `railway up`
# outright: the CLI uploads the WORKING TREE, not git, so every local build
# directory travels with it. On 2026-08-09 the upload died with "operation
# timed out" twice before the cause was found -- backend/build-int8 alone is
# 7.3 GB, and backend/sensen carries two more. Total payload was measured in
# multiple GB against a CLI that already fails at tens of MB.
#
# Anything matching a build directory must be listed here, and a new one
# appearing locally is a deploy outage waiting to happen -- hence the glob
# rather than three more literal paths.
# Literal paths are NOT sufficient here and listing them one by one is how
# this kept breaking: backend/external/SGEE/build is 1.2 GB of today's
# verification builds and matched none of the specific rules above it. Match
# any build directory at any depth instead -- no source directory in this tree
# is named `build`, so the glob is safe, and a build dir appearing somewhere
# new no longer silently becomes a deploy outage.
**/build
**/build-*
**/build_*
**/__pycache__
.next
out

# Editor / agent tooling
# Root-level prose only -- anchored with a leading slash ON PURPOSE.
#
# A bare `docs` matches a directory named `docs` at ANY depth, and on
# 2026-08-09 that silently broke the container build: SGEE grew a
# `tests/docs/` directory holding dsl_format_examples_test.cpp (the test that
# parses every example in the DSL reference, so a doc example cannot rot into
# unparseable syntax). Excluding it made CMake fail at configure with
# "Cannot find source file: docs/dsl_format_examples_test.cpp" -- a build
# break with no obvious connection to an ignore file.
#
# The lesson generalises: an unanchored directory name here is a trap that
# springs whenever anyone anywhere in the tree picks that name.
/docs
.playwright-mcp
.claude
config
# A DANGLING SYMLINK into ~/.gemini, which Railway's indexer follows and then
# fails on with "No such file or directory", aborting the upload before it
# starts. Agent tooling, never a build input.
**/.antigravitycli
**/.gemini

# Large non-build subtrees inside the vendored engines.
backend/external/SGEE/web-lsp
backend/external/SGEE/vscode-extension
backend/BigBrotherAnalytics
backend/sensen/external/CosyVoice
backend/sensen/eval_samples
backend/sensen/demo_qwen3

# sensen subtrees this build never configures. backend/CMakeLists.txt:139-146
# FORCEs BUILD_PYTHON_BINDINGS, BUILD_TESTS, BUILD_EXAMPLES and
# BUILD_BENCHMARKS to OFF, and sensen only calls add_subdirectory on each of
# them behind exactly those options (sensen/CMakeLists.txt:1281-1296), so none
# is read during configure. `docs` is documentation and is referenced by no
# CMake at all.
#
# This is not housekeeping. `railway up` enforces a fixed ~30 s deadline on the
# upload request, and at ~2.4 MB/s upstream a 62 MB payload does not fit: four
# consecutive deploys failed at 32 s with `operation timed out`, each leaving a
# stuck INITIALIZING deployment behind. These four directories are 18.5 MB of
# that 62 MB. Anything added here must be genuinely unreferenced by the build —
# `backend/external` was once excluded on the same reasoning and broke
# configure outright, because CMake did read it.
backend/sensen/benchmarks
backend/sensen/tests
backend/sensen/python
backend/sensen/examples
backend/sensen/docs
**/*.nsys-rep
**/*.sqlite
**/perf.data*
**/core.*

# calculator.assistant.StrategyAssistant's fine-tuned Qwen3-0.6B GGUF
# (Q8_0, ~639 MB) is fetched during the Docker BUILD from MODEL_URL, verified
# against MODEL_SHA256, and baked into the image there (backend/Dockerfile's
# `model` stage) -- it must never be present in this upload in the first
# place. At ~2.4 MB/s upstream this single file is roughly ten times the
# entire payload that already fails against `railway up`'s ~30 s deadline (see
# this file's own comment above), so there is no size of "just this once"
# that fits. Any .gguf found anywhere in the tree is excluded, not just an
# expected path, because a model dropped in for local testing is exactly the
# kind of file that is easy to forget is still sitting there at deploy time.
**/*.gguf

# Models are NOT staged into the upload: they are fetched at BUILD time from a
# private Railway bucket (SigV4, checksum-verified). Re-admitting a 639 MB file
# here only reproduces the measured HTTP 413 -- 619 MB compressed already failed.

# Editor/LSP caches. Never a build input, and backend/sensen/.cache/clangd
# alone is 4.5 MB against a ~30 s upload deadline.
**/.cache

# SGEE's PROSE docs, by explicit path. Note this is deliberately NOT a bare
# `docs` rule -- that is exactly what broke the build (see the /docs comment
# above): SGEE's tests/docs/ holds dsl_format_examples_test.cpp and MUST ship.
backend/external/SGEE/docs

# A screenshot committed at the repo root; not a build input.
current-ui.png

